        "In Scrabble, playing all seven tiles from your rack in a single turn, known as a Bingo, earns a 50-point bonus on top of the word's normal score. Racks containing common letters like R, S, T, L, N, and E are good candidates to scan for seven-letter combinations. Players often hold onto Q or Z waiting for the right combination, but Q can be played without a U in words like QI, QAT, and QAID, all valid in TWL and SOWPODS, which lets you use the tile without needing a specific follow-up letter.",
        "Playing your highest-scoring word isn't always the best move if it leaves you with an unplayable mix of tiles, such as four vowels. Considering the letters you'll be left with is part of a stronger long-term strategy, not just the immediate score. A hook is a single letter added to an existing word on the board to form a new one, for example adding an S to CORE to make SCORES while simultaneously building a word in the other direction — a standard way to generate extra value from tiles already in play. Short valid words like JO, OX, and ZA are useful in tight spaces on the board and worth memorising, since they come up more often than their obscurity suggests.",
        "A results list of every possible word isn't always useful on its own, so narrowing it down to what actually fits your situation is what makes a tool practical mid-game. Start by entering your available letters, then use a starts-with filter to match a specific board gap, an ends-with filter to connect to a tile already on the board, and a word-length filter to target a specific space or to look specifically for seven-letter Bingo plays. Filtering for specific letters is useful when you're holding a high-value tile like X, Z, or J and need to find a word that uses it, and the same filtering approach works for solving Wordle guesses or crossword clues.",
